::: {.doc-subject}

Remove all self-loops and duplicate edges, making the graph simple.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L3760-L3878){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.simplify()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Simplify](../assets/graphs/simplify.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Before: a four-node cycle with a self-loop on B and a second, parallel edge between C and D, both in the accent color. After simplify(), the self-loop and the duplicate edge are gone, shown dashed, leaving a simple four-node cycle." width="100%"}

A simple graph has no self-loops (edges from a node to itself) and no multi-edges (multiple
edges between the same pair of nodes). This method removes both types, which is a common
preprocessing step for algorithms that require simple graphs.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

`simplify()` is exactly `remove_selfloops()` followed by `remove_duplicate_edges()`
rolled into one call: self-loops disappear and each group of parallel edges collapses
to its earliest survivor (lowest edge ID). Directedness affects only the second
step, since $(u, v)$ and $(v, u)$ are distinct pairs in a directed graph but the same
pair when undirected. Once it returns, `is_simple()` is guaranteed to be `True`,
which is the condition many algorithms in the library quietly assume.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~remove_selfloops`) 

:   Remove only the self-loops.

[](`~remove_duplicate_edges`) 

:   Remove only the parallel edges.

[](`~is_simple`) 

:   Whether the graph has neither.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

`simplify()` combines `remove_selfloops()` and `remove_duplicate_edges()` in one call.
This graph starts with a duplicate "A"-"B" edge and two self-loops, and only the single
"A"-"B" edge remains:

::: {#ceff3287 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

g = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B", 1.0), ("A", "B", 2.0), ("A", "A"), ("B", "B")]
)
before = g.count_edges(), g.is_simple()

g.simplify()

before, (g.count_edges(), g.is_simple())
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
((4, False), (1, True))
```
:::
:::


### Cleaning a bank export

A bank's export has both problems: a payment listed twice and money moved between a
customer's own accounts. Duplicates inflate the number of payments between a pair, and
self-loops a node's degree, so both skew counts such as degree:

::: {#3834c533 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
export = [
    ("Acme", "Dee", 2600),
    ("Acme", "Eve", 2700),
    ("Acme", "Eve", 2700),
    ("Dee", "Grocer", 64),
    ("Dee", "Dee", 500),
    ("Dee", "Eve", 80),
    ("Eve", "Grocer", 38),
    ("Eve", "Grocer", 38),
]

bank = ec.Graph.from_edges(export, directed=True)
before = bank.get_degree("Eve"), bank.count_edges()

bank.simplify()

before, (bank.get_degree("Eve"), bank.count_edges())
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
((5, 8), (3, 5))
```
:::
:::


